I’ll inspect the specimen metadata, pattern, and the frozen analysis/tooling guidance first, then build and refine a complete multiphase GSAS-II model. I’ll keep the phase search anchored to coherent residual peak families and preserve the best physical checkpoint for the final deliverables.

The chemistry points first to Co–W–O phases: CoWO₄ is the ordinary 1:1 product, while 500 °C can leave WO₃ and/or Co₃O₄. I’m now extracting peak families and querying the frozen COD index for exact Co/W/O references before testing any less ordinary phase.

The observed peak positions already support only two coherent families: monoclinic γ-WO₃ (including its 23–25° triplet) and spinel Co₃O₄ (19.4°, 31.6°, 37.2°, 45.1°, 59.7°, 65.6° on the measured grid). There is no evident wolframite CoWO₄ 30–31° family. I’m setting up controlled GSAS-II candidate refinements now, including the close WO₃ reference variants, so phase choice is based on full-profile agreement and physical structure rather than peak matching alone.

The physically bounded refinement is stable at about 74.4 wt% WO₃ and 25.6 wt% Co₃O₄, essentially the stoichiometric unreacted-precursor ratio. Cells, crystallite sizes, and microstrain remain physical. I’m now mapping the residuals and refining the accepted structures’ symmetry-allowed coordinates; this is the appropriate next step because the remaining mismatch is mainly relative intensity, not unindexed peak position.

No additional coherent reflection family remains after the two-phase model. The residual instead supports ordinary specimen corrections: a [010] uniaxial microstrain term for WO₃ and a low-angle surface-roughness deficit; preferred-orientation tests returned ratios essentially 1.0 and were rejected. I’m tightening the correlated intensity corrections with bounded multistarts before the final profile polish.

The phase inventory is now stable: monoclinic γ-WO₃ and spinel Co₃O₄ account for every coherent reflection family; no CoWO₄ family is supported. I’m doing the final full-grid replay now so the CIFs, fractions, profile, statistics, and `final.gpx` all come from the same frozen state.

The residual maxima occur on already indexed WO₃/Co₃O₄ peaks and alternate in sign across them, which is characteristic of line-shape/intensity mismatch rather than a missing reflection family. I’m preserving the physically bounded model and will generate `fit.csv` with the same fresh full-grid forward replay the verifier uses, rather than relying on cached GSAS-II arrays.

